{"name":"Echo Iris","hex":"#b6dff4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"echo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Echo of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A reflected sound that is heard again by its initial observer.","An utterance repeating what has just been said."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/echo"},{"term":"iris","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of the genus Iris, common in the northern hemisphere, and generally having attractive blooms (See  Iris (plant) on  Wikipedia.Wikipedia ).","The contractile membrane perforated by the pupil, which adjusts to control the amount of light reaching the retina, and which forms the colored portion of the eye (See  Iris (anatomy) on  Wikipedia.Wikipedia )."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To open or close in the manner of an iris.","To cause (something) to shine with the colours of the rainbow; to make iridescent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iris"}],"entries":[{"title":"Iris (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"In ancient Greek religion and mythology, Iris is a daughter of the gods Thaumas and Electra, and the personification of the rainbow. She functions as a messenger and servant to the Olympians, particularly Hera. Iris was traditionally seen as the consort of Zephyrus, the god of the west wind and one of the four Anemoi, by whom she is the mother of Pothos in some texts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Iris_Louvre_L43_n2.jpg/330px-Iris_Louvre_L43_n2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_(mythology)"},{"title":"Echo","blocks":[{"text":"In audio signal processing and acoustics, an echo is a reflection of sound that arrives at the listener with a delay after the direct sound. The delay is directly proportional to the distance of the reflecting surface from the source and the listener. Typical examples are the echo produced by the bottom of a well, a building, or the walls of enclosed and empty rooms."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Echo"},{"title":"Iris (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Iris is an ambiguous color term, usually referring to shades ranging from blue-violet to violet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Unidentified_Iris_Chanticleer_Purple_2868px.jpg/330px-Unidentified_Iris_Chanticleer_Purple_2868px.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Winged_goddess_Cdm_Paris_392.jpg/500px-Winged_goddess_Cdm_Paris_392.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Winged_goddess_Cdm_Paris_392.jpg/1280px-Winged_goddess_Cdm_Paris_392.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Winged_goddess_Cdm_Paris_392.jpg/500px-Winged_goddess_Cdm_Paris_392.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Winged female figure holding a caduceus: Iris (messenger of the gods) or Nike (Victory)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWinged_goddess_Cdm_Paris_392.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Pompeii_-_Casa_dei_Vettii_-_Ixion.jpg/500px-Pompeii_-_Casa_dei_Vettii_-_Ixion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Pompeii_-_Casa_dei_Vettii_-_Ixion.jpg/1280px-Pompeii_-_Casa_dei_Vettii_-_Ixion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Pompeii_-_Casa_dei_Vettii_-_Ixion.jpg/500px-Pompeii_-_C